1466 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Pliara recognizes dwarves as its very first recorded humanoid inhabitants\054 and they remain legendary for their physical power and immovable resolve\056 Standing between four and five feet tall and weighing around one hundred fifty pounds\054 these stocky folk possess broad frames and natural endurance that help them survive the toughest working conditions\056 Most dwarves organize themselves into sprawling\054 tightly knit clans that work together to forge vast underground settlements or build homes on mountain cliffs\056 Clan loyalty runs extremely deep\054 meaning that an insult directed at one dwarf is treated as a direct slight against their entire family line\056 Despite keeping to themselves most of the time\054 they occasionally enter into business arrangements with outside species\054 such as trading high quality smithing work to neighboring orcs in exchange for long distance transport\056\n\nThe physical traits of dwarven folk change drastically across the different corners of Pliara\056 Hill dwarves are the most widespread group across populated cities\054 carrying a proud\054 noble presence as they manage large clans devoted to mining\054 hunting\054 or forging\056 High in the crags live the range dwarves\054 who stand slightly taller at three and a half feet and sport strikingly curly red or brown facial hair\056 Tucked away in the wild forests of The Wild Hook are the tiny canopy dwarves\054 who grow to less than three feet tall and blend into the hollow trunks of Jiila trees\056 The isolated marsh dwarves present a far darker look\054 featuring pale blue or sickly green skin\054 glowing orange eyes\054 yellowed fingernails\054 and messy hair filled with swamp debris\056\n\nDwarven society revolves heavily around specialized labor\054 craft traditions\054 and honored family roots\056 Well known to exemplify Dwarven culture is the Ironmolder Clan\054 a welcoming family lineage that founded the village of New Heights to raise fostered children from various backgrounds\056 Experienced dwarven artisans often pass down their trade through strict apprenticeships\054 teaching their students how to handle stonework\054 masonry\054 and metalsmithing\056 While isolated groups like the chaotic marsh dwarves avoid civilization entirely\054 most dwarven clans remain dedicated to protecting their homes\054 honoring their leaders\054 and preserving ancient traditions passed down across centuries\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 1467 0 R
     /Pg 1434 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

